*** Resetting replication at the very beginning of the test.

*** Generating a configuration file in JSON format for the Binlog
*** Server utility.

*** Determining binlog file directory from the server.

*** Creating a temporary directory <BINSRV_STORAGE_PATH> for storing
*** binlog files downloaded via the Binlog Server utility.

*** Phase 1: writing events into binlog N on the source.
CREATE DATABASE test1;
CREATE TABLE test1.t(id INT UNSIGNED NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY) ENGINE=InnoDB;
INSERT INTO test1.t VALUES (1), (2), (3);

*** Gracefully restarting the source. This writes a STOP event as the
*** last event of binlog N (not a ROTATE) and opens binlog N+1, which
*** on the source starts with only its FORMAT_DESCRIPTION /
*** PREVIOUS_GTIDS header.
# restart

*** Determining the binary log name after restart (binlog N+1).

*** Starting Binlog Server Utility in background in pull mode. On a
*** fresh storage it collects binlog N (finalized at its STOP event)
*** and opens binlog N+1 writing only the 4-byte magic; N+1's
*** FORMAT_DESCRIPTION stays buffered (no transaction boundary), so N+1
*** remains exactly 4 bytes on storage.

*** Waiting for the replication source listener port ready to accept
*** connections.

*** Starting a downstream mysqlbinlog --read-from-remote-server
*** --stop-never session requesting binlog N. --stop-never keeps the
*** connection open so PBS delivers events as they appear instead of
*** disconnecting on EOF.

*** Waiting for PBS to report that the sender reached EOF. By this point
*** the downstream has consumed all of binlog N (through its STOP event)
*** and the sender is parked at the N -> N+1 boundary with N+1 still
*** holding only the magic - the exact window this test targets.
include/wait_for_pattern_in_file.inc [sender : fetched EOF]

*** Phase 2: a single transaction into binlog N+1 on the source. This
*** is the first transaction boundary in N+1, so PBS now flushes N+1
*** (its FORMAT_DESCRIPTION and these events) to storage and the parked
*** downstream rolls into it.
INSERT INTO test1.t VALUES (4), (5), (6);

*** The downstream stream must now carry an (artificial) ROTATE naming
*** binlog N+1 ahead of N+1's events. Without the fix this ROTATE
*** is never emitted (N+1's events stream as a continuation of N) and
*** this wait times out, failing the test.
include/wait_for_pattern_in_file.inc [Rotate to binlog.000002]

*** Sending SIGTERM to the downstream mysqlbinlog session and waiting
*** for it to terminate.

*** Dropping the test database.
DROP DATABASE test1;

*** Sending SIGTERM signal to the Binlog Server Utility and waiting
*** for the process to terminate.

*** Removing the Binlog Server utility storage directory.

*** Removing the Binlog Server utility log file.

*** Removing the Binlog Server utility configuration file.
